@code_type rust .rs
@comment_type // %s

@title LaTeX

@s The Distance Section in LaTeX

Woven Document prepared the `Distance` input as one section containing a
title, prose, the equation $d^2 = x^2 + y^2$, a reference to `distance.rs`,
and that root code definition. Weaver showed how HTML represents those facts.
The LaTeX backend receives the same facts and chooses different output syntax:

```text
document title       -> \title{Distance}
section 1 anchor     -> \hypertarget{litweb-1-1}{}
inline equation      -> \(d^2 = x^2 + y^2\)
reference target     -> \hyperlink{litweb-1-1}{1}
displayed Rust       -> LitwebCode environment
```

The renderer does not rediscover the section or resolve the block name. It
only expresses the prepared meaning in LaTeX and escapes authored text for
the output context where that text appears.

For the standalone input `distance.lit`, planning produces two files without
running TeX:

```text
distance.tex
litweb-latex/litweb.sty
```

`distance.tex` contains the document and refers to the relative style path.
The packaged style supplies black body typography with blue clickable links,
linked section notation, and fixed-width code that wraps instead of
disappearing beyond the page. Its 6.5-inch-wide by 8.7-inch-tall text area
follows CWEB's default dimensions instead of LaTeX's substantially narrower
book default. A caller may later compile the pair with LuaLaTeX; Litweb's
planning operation itself remains an ordinary deterministic weave.

--- src/latex.rs
//! Deterministic LaTeX generation from parsed `.lit` programs.

